{
  "version": 3,
  "description": "Behaviour contract for the Thai Kedmanee <-> US QWERTY converter. Any implementation claiming to be this app must reproduce every case exactly. The mapping table was dumped from macOS layout data (see Tools/dump-kedmanee.swift); the mixed-mode cases encode the orthographic gate, including its documented limitations.",
  "schema": {
    "version": "Bumped whenever the shape of this file changes. Readers must refuse a version they do not understand.",
    "mapping": "The whole Kedmanee <-> US QWERTY key table, as { qwerty, kedmanee } pairs. Each value is exactly ONE Unicode scalar, and the table is a bijection. Verify this before running any case: a port is most likely to go wrong here.",
    "cases": "Behaviour cases: { name, mode, input, output }. Feed `input` to the converter in `mode`; the result must equal `output` exactly, compared as Unicode scalars.",
    "modes": {
      "englishToThai": "Mechanical whole-string flip, QWERTY keystrokes -> Kedmanee. Never consults the gate.",
      "thaiToEnglish": "Mechanical whole-string flip, the other way. Never consults the gate.",
      "mixed": "Per-run judgement: convert a run only if it is malformed in its own script. This is where precision and recall live, and where a port will differ if its gate differs."
    },
    "notes": [
      "Walk unicodeScalars, never grapheme clusters: Thai combining marks fuse with the consonant before them, so a per-character loop is handed clusters that appear in no table.",
      "Cases named 'precision:' are the promise that correct text is never mangled. They matter more than the 'recall:' cases — mangling correct text is a worse failure than leaving a mistyping, because the user can see and fix the latter.",
      "Cases named 'known miss:' record text the mixed gate deliberately leaves alone. A port that 'fixes' them has almost certainly broken a precision case; check both before celebrating.",
      "Cases named 'substitution:' cover characters the OS swapped in for a keystroke. A port that skips them still converts typed text on a machine with smart quotes off, then fails the moment the user pastes."
    ],
    "typographicSubstitutes": "Characters an operating system inserts in place of a keystroke: { substitute, key } pairs, each exactly ONE Unicode scalar. macOS turns a typed ' into U+2019, \" into U+201C/D and - into U+2013, and text pasted from other apps arrives the same way. Fold a substitute onto its `key` ONLY in the QWERTY -> Kedmanee direction, and ONLY for text you are actually converting: these keys carry ' = NGO NGU, \" = '.', - = KHO KHWAI, so without the fold those Kedmanee characters are unreachable. The fold is many-to-one and has no inverse; the Kedmanee -> QWERTY direction always emits the straight ASCII key. Text the gate leaves alone must keep its original characters -- straightening a curl is a change to text that was promised to be untouched."
  },
